About

    This subdirectory contains a patch that
    - exposes AGAMA's Fortran interface to Ramses (similar to ../agama)
    - adds functionality to AGAMA's Fortran interface to an existing AGAMA installation without the need to modify it (i.e. edit source and recompile)
    For now, this additional functionality is limited to 'destroying' a previously allocated potential object with the intent to free the memory it uses. Note that the difference is minimal to non-existent with respect to not deleting it. Still, this patch is a neat, useful illustration of how to 'patch' an exisiting AGAMA installation and its Fortran interface.

    IMPORTANT: The AGAMA library must be compiled on the SAME system that this patch is meant to be run!


Usage

    Compile Ramses patch with either of:

        - Makefile_AGAMA_INTERFACE_MULTIPATCH
        - Makefile_AGAMA_INTERFACE_DICE_MULTIPATCH

    In the NML, include the following block:

    ! file path is set by initfile (in INIT_PARAMS):
    &AGAMA_PARAMS
    agama_file = 'some_agama_potential.ini'
    agama_debug = .true.       ! overrides agama_verbose
    agama_verbose = .true.     ! better to set to .true. (:default)
    agama_file = 0.0           ! controls the file read frequency (default: 1e10)
    /

    See Notes below.

Notes
    - agama_file is either an INI file with [Potential] section, either specifing an analytic potential or  with a potential approximation and derivatives (with its coefficients, etc.)
    - agama_debug is particularly useful for debugging; when set to .true., it shows when potential objects are created (and deleted, if chosen to do so).
    - the file is read at every t_step; the default value of t_step = 1e10 means the file is only read at t = 0.


